<h1>Lesson 6: Preparing for the Final Project</h1>

<div style="display: inline-block; background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #1F1976; font-weight: bold; padding: 4px 12px; border-radius: 10px; margin-bottom: 12px;">Aligns to curriculum Lesson 12</div>

<p>This lesson prepares you to teach one curriculum lesson:</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
<p><b>Curriculum Lesson 12:</b> Reactive Logic Project</p>
<a href="[LESSON 12 URL]" style="display: inline-block; background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; padding: 6px 14px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; margin-top: 6px; font-size: 13px;">View Curriculum Lesson 12</a>
</div>

<hr>

<p><b>In this lesson, you'll:</b></p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 6px 0;">• Review the project's goals and four-milestone structure</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 6px 0;">• Learn the TAG feedback method students use with each other</p>
<p style="margin: 0;">• Practice grading a real mock project against the rubric</p>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #222222; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
<p><b>💡 Notice as you explore:</b> the rubric's five categories are graded independently, a weak plan doesn't automatically mean weak code, and your professional judgment is always the final call, not a single calibrated answer.</p>
</div>

<hr>

